
If your router doesnt support CONE NAT: (e.g. default linux with SYMMETRICAL NAT):

    On Socks5 proxy server

        run Socks5 proxy server behind NAT on a LAN IP

        set it to rewrite UDP ASSOC hostname to ext IP of the router
            "REWRITE_UDP_ASSOCIATE_IP_TO": "3.3.3.3"

        set it to bind UDP port range e.g. 20000..21000 
            "MIN_PORT_UDP_ASSOCIATE": 20000
            "MAX_PORT_UDP_ASSOCIATE": 21000

    On router 
        forward UDP  20000..21000 to the LAN IP of the Socks5 proxy server
        forward TCP proxy port e.g. 5001  to the LAN IP of the Socks5 proxy server

        e.g for Linux router : 

            iptables -t nat -A PREROUTING -d modus.tanatos.org -p udp -m multiport  --dports 20000:20100    -j DNAT --to  172.22.24.106
            iptables -t nat -A PREROUTING -d modus.tanatos.org -p tcp --dport 5101 -j DNAT --to  172.22.24.106
            iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -d 172.22.24.106 -j MASQUERADE
            sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1

            where

            modus.tanatos.org           ext IP of the router
            172.22.24.106               LAN IP of proxy server
            20000:20100                 UDP port range of that Socks5 proxy server
            5101                        TCP port of that Socks5 proxy server

            
If your router support CONE NAT (Open\DD-WRT, TPlink, ASUS,etc):

    On Socks5 proxy server

        run Socks5 proxy server behind NAT on a LAN IP

        set it to rewrite UDP ASSOC hostname to ext IP of the router
            "REWRITE_UDP_ASSOCIATE_IP_TO": "3.3.3.3"

        set CONE NAT FIX
            "CONE_NAT_FIX": true

    On router 
    
        forward TCP proxy port e.g. 5001  to the LAN IP of the Socks5 proxy server
        enable CONE NAT


